{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly upward) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude spikes (e.g., 70 to 76) followed by sharp corrections. Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges rather than point estimates.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows a value of 1.00 (positive shock), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent price surge. Adjust forecasts upward immediately following positive sentiment, but apply a stronger downward correction in the 5-10 day horizon post-spike to account for the rapid pullback observed in the data.",
    "Eliminate persistent upward drift bias: The model exhibits a systematic bias to drift prices higher over the 30-day horizon regardless of the actual downward trend in the latter half of the forecast window. Force the long-term forecast (days 15-30) to revert to the recent 18-day historical mean rather than extrapolating the initial trend.",
    "Lag correction for trend reversals: The model reacts too slowly to trend changes, often continuing to forecast increases while the market has already peaked and begun falling. Introduce a lag-adjustment factor that reduces the forecast slope by 50% once the 3-day rolling average of true outcomes turns negative.",
    "Sentiment-weighted overshoot penalty: When sentiment is neutral (0.00) but the preceding sentiment was highly positive (1.00), the model fails to price in the 'hangover' effect (price drop). Apply a negative adjustment of 2-3 EUR to the forecast for days 3-7 following a positive sentiment event."
  ]
}
